     hhttttppdd..ccoonnff — HTTP daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     hhttttppdd..ccoonnff is the configuration file for the HTTP daemon, httpd(8).

     hhttttppdd..ccoonnff is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for httpd(8).

     _S_e_r_v_e_r_s
           Listening HTTP web servers.

     _T_y_p_e_s
           Media types and extensions.

     Within the sections, a host _a_d_d_r_e_s_s can be specified by IPv4 address,
     IPv6 address, interface name, interface group, or DNS hostname.  If the
     address is an interface name, httpd(8) will look up the first IPv4 ad‐
     dress and any other IPv4 and IPv6 addresses of the specified network in‐
     terface.  If ‘*’ is given as an address, httpd(8) will listen on all IPv4
     and IPv6 addresses.  _0_._0_._0_._0 means to listen on all IPv4 addresses and _:_:
     all IPv6 addresses.  A _p_o_r_t can be specified by number or name.  The port
     name to number mappings are found in the file _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s; see
     services(5) for details.

     The current line can be extended over multiple lines using a backslash
     (‘\’).  Comments can be put anywhere in the file using a hash mark (‘#’),
     and extend to the end of the current line.  Care should be taken when
     commenting out multi-line text: the comment is effective until the end of
     the entire block.

     Argument names not beginning with a letter, digit, or underscore must be
     quoted.

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword,
     for example:

           include "/etc/httpd.conf.local"

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, ddiirreeccttoorryy, lloogg, or rroooott).  Within unquoted arguments,
     the string $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           ext_ip="10.0.0.1"
           server "example.com" {
                   listen on $ext_ip port 80
           }

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Here are the settings that can be set globally:

     cchhrroooott _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Set the chroot(2) directory.  If not specified, it defaults to
             _/_v_a_r_/_w_w_w, the home directory of the www user.

     ddeeffaauulltt ttyyppee _t_y_p_e_/_s_u_b_t_y_p_e
             Set the default media type that is used if the media type for a
             specified extension is not found in the configured types or for
             files without a file extension; see the _T_y_p_e_s section below.  If
             not specified, the default type is set to
             _a_p_p_l_i_c_a_t_i_o_n_/_o_c_t_e_t_-_s_t_r_e_a_m.

     eerrrrddooccss _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Let httpd(8) return custom error documents instead of the built-
             in ones.

             _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y is relative to the cchhrroooott.

             Custom error documents are standalone “.html” files provided in
             one of the following ways:
                   ••   As HTML files named after the 3-digit HTTP response
                       code they are used for, e.g., _4_0_4_._h_t_m_l.
                   ••   As a generic template file named _e_r_r_._h_t_m_l which is used
                       for response codes no dedicated file is provided for.

             In case the latter does not exist and there is no dedicated file
             available for a certain response code, the built-in error docu‐
             ment will be used as fallback.

             A custom error document may contain the following macros that
             will be expanded at runtime:

                   $$HHTTTTPP__EERRRROORR     The error message text.
                   $$RREESSPPOONNSSEE__CCOODDEE  The 3-digit HTTP response code sent to the
                                   client.
                   $$SSEERRVVEERR__SSOOFFTTWWAARREE
                                   The server software name of httpd(8).

     llooggddiirr _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Specifies the full path of the directory in which log files will
             be written.  If not specified, it defaults to _/_l_o_g_s within the
             chroot(2) directory.

     pprreeffoorrkk _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Run the specified number of server processes.  This increases the
             performance and prevents delays when connecting to a server.
             httpd(8) runs 3 server processes by default.

   SSeerrvveerrss
     The configured web servers.

     Each sseerrvveerr section starts with a declaration of the server _n_a_m_e.  If a
     request does not match any server name, it is handled by the first de‐
     fined sseerrvveerr section that matches the listening port.

     sseerrvveerr _n_a_m_e {...}
             Match the server name using shell globbing rules, see glob(7).
             This can be an explicit name, _w_w_w_._e_x_a_m_p_l_e_._c_o_m, or a name includ‐
             ing wildcards, _*_._e_x_a_m_p_l_e_._c_o_m.

     sseerrvveerr mmaattcchh _n_a_m_e {...}
             Match the server name using pattern matching, see patterns(7).

     Followed by a block of options enclosed in curly braces:

     aalliiaass _n_a_m_e
             Specify an additional alias _n_a_m_e for this server.

     aalliiaass mmaattcchh _n_a_m_e
             Like the aalliiaass option, but mmaattcchh the _n_a_m_e using pattern matching
             instead of shell globbing rules, see patterns(7).

     [nnoo] aauutthheennttiiccaattee [_r_e_a_l_m] wwiitthh _h_t_p_a_s_s_w_d
             Authenticate a remote user for _r_e_a_l_m by checking the credentials
             against the user authentication file _h_t_p_a_s_s_w_d.  The file name is
             relative to the cchhrroooott and must be readable by the www user.  Use
             the nnoo aauutthheennttiiccaattee directive to disable authentication in a lo‐
             cation.

     bblloocckk ddrroopp
             Drop the connection without sending an error page.

     bblloocckk [rreettuurrnn _c_o_d_e [_u_r_i]]
             Close the connection and send an error page.  If the optional re‐
             turn code is not specified, httpd(8) denies access with a ‘403
             Forbidden’ response.  The optional _u_r_i argument can be used with
             return codes in the 3xx range to send an HTTP Location header for
             redirection to a specified URI.

             It is possible to rewrite the request to redirect it to a differ‐
             ent external location.  The _u_r_i may contain predefined macros
             that will be expanded at runtime:

                   $$DDOOCCUUMMEENNTT__UURRII  The request path.
                   $$QQUUEERRYY__SSTTRRIINNGG  The query string of the request.
                   $$QQUUEERRYY__SSTTRRIINNGG__EENNCC
                                  The URL-encoded query string of the request.
                   $$RREEMMOOTTEE__AADDDDRR   The IP address of the connected client.
                   $$RREEMMOOTTEE__PPOORRTT   The TCP source port of the connected client.
                   $$RREEMMOOTTEE__UUSSEERR   The remote user for HTTP authentication.
                   $$RREEQQUUEESSTT__SSCCHHEEMMEE
                                  The request scheme (http or https).
                   $$RREEQQUUEESSTT__UURRII   The request path and optional query string.
                   $$SSEERRVVEERR__AADDDDRR   The configured IP address of the server.
                   $$SSEERRVVEERR__PPOORRTT   The configured TCP port of the server.
                   $$SSEERRVVEERR__NNAAMMEE   The name of the server.
                   $$HHTTTTPP__HHOOSSTT     The host from the HTTP Host header.
                   %_n             The capture index _n of a string that was
                                  captured by the enclosing llooccaattiioonn mmaattcchh op‐
                                  tion.

     ccoonnnneeccttiioonn _o_p_t_i_o_n
             Set the specified options and limits for HTTP connections.  Mul‐
             tiple options may be specified within curly braces.  Valid op‐
             tions are:

             mmaaxx rreeqquueesstt bbooddyy _n_u_m_b_e_r
                     Set the maximum body size in bytes that the client can
                     send to the server.  The default value is 1048576 bytes
                     (1M).

             mmaaxx rreeqquueessttss _n_u_m_b_e_r
                     Set the maximum number of requests per persistent HTTP
                     connection.  Persistent connections are negotiated using
                     the Keep-Alive header in HTTP/1.0 and enabled by default
                     in HTTP/1.1.  The default maximum number of requests per
                     connection is 100.

             rreeqquueesstt ttiimmeeoouutt _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
                     Specify the inactivity timeout for HTTP operations be‐
                     tween client and server, for example the maximum time to
                     wait for a request from the client.  The default timeout
                     is 60 seconds (1 minute).  The maximum is 2147483647 sec‐
                     onds (68 years).

             ttiimmeeoouutt _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
                     Specify the inactivity timeout in seconds for accepted
                     sessions, for example the maximum time to wait for I/O
                     from the FastCGI backend.  The default timeout is 600
                     seconds (10 minutes).  The maximum is 2147483647 seconds
                     (68 years).

